try  who's got shovel ready projects ready to  go with an opportunity to apply  and get connected within the next year  the goal is to get  98 of canadian homes connected to  high-speed internet by the year 2026.  high-speed internet is defined as 50  megabit per second download speed  and 10 megabit upload speed and the  pandemic has exposed canada's digital  divide  there are people that can't work from  home they can't actually do their  education from home  they don't have the option of having a  video conference with their doctor for a  prescription but have to go into the  office consecutive governments have made  similar broadband promises all the way  back to 2008.  monday's announcement was made 18 months  ago by the liberals and opposition  parties say  the government is dragging its feet what  we're hearing from a lot of smaller  internet service providers that they've  already lost this year  and they're not going to be able to get  shovels in the ground until some point  next year  after the winter that could delay the  plan to have high speed internet in  every home  by the year 2030 mike lecouter global  news ottawa  next the canadian who returned from the  first world war only to face the grief  of a pandemic  remembrance day is going to look  different this year people won't be able  to gather the way they usually do  but families across this country will  still honor those who fought and never  came home  more than a century ago it was not only  the first world war that led to millions  of deaths  but a flu pandemic and for some families  it was a double blow  redmond shannon has the story of one of  those families  so this is a picture of of james and  ellen  taken in 1916. genealogist  eunice robinson lives in the past unlike  her husband barry  my husband is the original non-gen and  the non-gen is the  term i coined to say somebody who could  absolutely care less about genealogy  but exploring barry's family history is  one of the most  interesting projects she's worked on in  1907 his great-grandfather james baird  brown left scotland with his wife and  children  to start a new life in winnipeg seven  years later  war broke out and their sons james jr  and george  enlisted with canada's 44th battalion  their father then 53 wanted to fight  too so he lied about his age saying he  was  43. you hear about the youngers often  saying they're older  but rarely do you hear about the older  saying that they're younger so  i guess he felt extremely patriotic i  believe when he finally got  to england they thought hey you're too  old fella  so they put him to work in the  quartermaster's office where i guess he  did quite a good job  in october 1916 george was killed in  battle  one of the more than 24 000 canadian  deaths  in the brutal and infamous psalm  offensive  george is buried at the adenac military  cemetery in northern france  before the war was over james senior by  then aged 55  was discharged for being over age and  returned to winnipeg  just weeks after the war ended james jr  married a british war bride tying the  knot at this  church in london around that time james  senior  returned to europe to find his younger  son's grave  while in london he most likely stayed on  this street at james junior's home  but he contracted influenza during the  spanish flu pandemic that was killing  millions  and he died aged 56. who can envision  what the world would have been like with  a previous pandemic  and this has really brought it home and  you kind of wonder  where you really are maybe put in a  position where  you have no control over getting this  disease  james jr returned to winnipeg two years  later  he died there in 1953 taking with him  the answer to a mystery about his dad's  final journey to europe i don't really  know if he ever found george's graveyard  that was done under the canadian war  graves commission so  a good chance that he may not have i  don't really know all those answers and  unfortunately i don't think there's  anyone living that still does  perhaps it's enough to know that james  senior paid the ultimate price trying to  find his son's final resting place  redmond shannon global news london  and that is global national for this  monday i'm donna friesen tonight here  canada is the sun  starting to set over vancouver thanks  for watching hope to see you here again  tomorrow  take care of yourselves bye-bye  [Music]  you
 

